# cdctl reference

Manage a Control D account over its REST API.

cdctl is not ctrld: ctrld is Control D's DNS daemon and runs DNS on a machine; cdctl manages the account behind it. They coexist.

## Global flags

- `-p, --profile <PK|name>` — Profile to operate on (name or PK) (env: `CONTROLD_PROFILE`)
- `--json` — Emit JSON (full document) instead of a table
- `--fields <a,b>` — Comma-separated JSON fields to keep; implies --json
- `--plain` — Tables without borders, for awk/cut
- `-y, --yes` — Skip confirmation prompts (ignored when the target profile is implicit)
- `--no-retry` — Disable automatic retries (GETs only ever retry)
- `--timeout <SECS>` — Per-request timeout in seconds
- `--debug` — Request/response trace on stderr; token always redacted

## cdctl api

Raw request against the API origin (escape hatch; GET by default)

- `<path>` — Path relative to the API origin, query string included (e.g. '/access?device_id=abc')
- `-X, --method <METHOD>` — HTTP method; non-GET also requires --yes
- `-F, --field <key=value>` — Form field (repeatable); keys are sent literally — write 'hostnames[]=a' yourself. Requires a non-GET -X
- `--input <->` — Read a JSON body from stdin, sent verbatim; "-" is the only accepted value. Requires a non-GET -X

## cdctl auth

Authenticate cdctl with an API token

### cdctl auth login

Store an API token read from stdin

- `--token-stdin` — Read the token from stdin

### cdctl auth status

Whether a token is configured, where from, and who it authenticates

### cdctl auth logout

Remove the stored token for the active context

## cdctl profile

Inspect the account's profiles

### cdctl profile list

List profiles (RULES counts enabled rules only)

### cdctl profile get

Show one profile by id or name

- `<SELECTOR>` — Profile id or name (names resolve case-insensitively; ambiguity is an error)

## cdctl folder

Manage a profile's rule folders (API: groups)

### cdctl folder list

List a profile's folders

### cdctl folder create

Create a folder (omitting --action makes an action-less folder)

- `<NAME>` — Folder name
- `--action <ACTION>` — Action to apply
- `--via <IP|CNAME|proxy>` — Spoof target (IP or CNAME), or the redirect proxy PK (see `proxy list`)
- `--via6 <IPv6>` — Spoof-only IPv6 target; rejected on folders and the profile default (D16)
- `--enabled` — Enable
- `--disabled` — Disable
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

### cdctl folder update

Rename a folder or change its action (sends only the flags given)

- `<SELECTOR>` — Folder id or name (names resolve case-insensitively; ambiguity is an error)
- `--name <NAME>` — New name
- `--action <ACTION>` — Action to apply
- `--via <IP|CNAME|proxy>` — Spoof target (IP or CNAME), or the redirect proxy PK (see `proxy list`)
- `--via6 <IPv6>` — Spoof-only IPv6 target; rejected on folders and the profile default (D16)
- `--enabled` — Enable
- `--disabled` — Disable
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

### cdctl folder delete

Delete a folder and its contained rules

- `<SELECTOR>` — Folder id or name (names resolve case-insensitively; ambiguity is an error)
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

## cdctl rule

Manage a profile's DNS rules

### cdctl rule list

List a profile's rules

- `--folder <id|name>` — Folder id or name; omitted lists the whole profile (never a `folder_id=0` segment — read-verification.md section 3)

### cdctl rule create

Create rules (action required; at most 500 hostnames per invocation)

- `<HOSTNAME>` — Hostnames to create (wildcards like *.example.com are legal)
- `--action <ACTION>` — Action to apply
- `--via <IP|CNAME|proxy>` — Spoof target (IP or CNAME), or the redirect proxy PK (see `proxy list`)
- `--via6 <IPv6>` — Spoof-only IPv6 target; rejected on folders and the profile default (D16)
- `--enabled` — Enable
- `--disabled` — Disable
- `--folder <id|name>` — Folder id or name to create the rules in
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

### cdctl rule update

Update rules (sends only the flags given — `PUT /rules` merges)

- `<HOSTNAME>`
- `--action <ACTION>` — Action to apply
- `--via <IP|CNAME|proxy>` — Spoof target (IP or CNAME), or the redirect proxy PK (see `proxy list`)
- `--via6 <IPv6>` — Spoof-only IPv6 target; rejected on folders and the profile default (D16)
- `--enabled` — Enable
- `--disabled` — Disable
- `--folder <id|name>` — Move to this folder (id or name)
- `--root` — Move back to the root, out of any folder
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

### cdctl rule delete

Delete rules

- `<HOSTNAME>`
- `-n, --dry-run` — Print the withheld mutation as a plan; write nothing

## cdctl config

Read and write cdctl's own configuration

### cdctl config get

Print one value (raw, not JSON)

- `<KEY>`

### cdctl config set

Set one value

- `<KEY>`
- `<VALUE>`

### cdctl config list

The active context, each value annotated with its source

### cdctl config path

The config file path

## cdctl completions

Shell completion script (works without a token)

- `<SHELL>`

## cdctl reference

The full command surface as one Markdown document (works without a token)
